<h1>Andrea M. Buford</h1>
<p>Judge up for retention, on the Chicago ballot November 3, 2026. Incumbent.</p>
<p>Circuit Court of Cook County</p>
<h2>Ratings</h2>
<ul>
<li>Chicago Bar Association: <a href="https://www.chicagobar.org/votejudges#:~:text=Andrea%20M.%20Buford" rel="nofollow noopener">Qualified</a></li>
<li>Illinois State Bar Association: <a href="https://www.isba.org/judicial-elections/counties/cook#:~:text=Andrea%20M.%20Buford" rel="nofollow noopener">Yes</a></li>
</ul>
<h2>Record</h2>
<p>Appointed to the Circuit Court in 2013 and elected in 2014; presiding judge of the Child Protection Division, where she has spent most of her career hearing child abuse and neglect cases. Injustice Watch reports that as a lawyer for the city she represented former Chicago police officer Joseph Miedzianowski, by then convicted of racketeering and drug conspiracy, in a 2007 federal civil rights trial brought by two former federal agents who said the department retaliated against them for trying to report him; the jury awarded the agents $9.75 million. She has served as president of the Cook County Bar Association and the Illinois Judicial Council. A judge stays on the bench with a yes from 60 percent of voters.</p>
<h2>Background</h2>
<p>Attorney in private practice representing corporations and municipalities in civil litigation (1987-2013), while also a hearing officer for the Illinois State Board of Education and Department of Human Services (2009-2013) and a commissioner on the Illinois Court of Claims (2007-2013). Law degree from IIT Chicago-Kent (1986).</p>
